@@ -0,0 +1,20 @@
+# 0001 — Reservation expiry is evaluated lazily from the clock
+
+Date: 2026-10-29 · Status: accepted
+
+## Context
+Holds nobody confirms must lapse after their TTL. The package has no I/O and no background job;
+all time comes from the injected `Clock`.
+
+## Decision
+Expiry is derived, not scheduled. An `ACTIVE` reservation with `now >= expires_at` is treated as
+`EXPIRED` everywhere: `held()`/`available()` ignore it, and any read through the service
+(`get_reservation`, `confirm_reservation`, `release_reservation`, an idempotent `reserve` replay)
+persists the `EXPIRED` status. No sweep exists or is needed.
+
+## Consequences
+- Every new query over reservations must apply the same `is_lapsed(now)` rule rather than trusting
+  the stored status alone.
+- A database-backed repository must keep expiry evaluable at read time (store `expires_at`, filter
+  by it) — it cannot rely on a job flipping statuses.
+- Only `ACTIVE` holds can lapse; `CONFIRMED` and `RELEASED` are terminal.
